Subject: Run Redis under Fil-C
Origin: Filnix
Upstream-Status: Not submitted

- Encoded reply buffers pack a 9-byte payloadHeader before each chunk, so
  the robj pointer in a bulkStrRef payload sits at an arbitrary offset.
  Fil-C loads pointers only from 8-byte-aligned addresses and trapped in
  computeUnsharedReplyBytes and processEncodedBufferForWrite. Under Fil-C,
  the header is unpacked (16 bytes), clientReplyBlock.buf is 8-aligned and
  every payload is padded to 8 bytes (REPLY_PAYLOAD_SPACE); the walks over
  encoded buffers step by the padded size.
- The client tracking prefix table and the blocked-client timeout table use
  client pointers as rax key bytes; a pointer read back from bytes has no
  capability. Clients are registered in an exact pointer table when
  created, and clientFromBytes() looks them up.
- pqsort swapped aligned elements as longs, dropping the capabilities of
  the pointers in them (GEORADIUS ... COUNT sorts geoPoints). Swap as
  void * under Fil-C.
- With jemalloc, dismissMemory() madvises heap pages after fork. Fil-C
  refuses madvise on its heap; skip it, as Redis does for libc malloc.
- Fil-C's glibc issues sync_file_range() as a raw cancellable syscall,
  which the runtime refuses; use fsync() instead.
- Build without backtrace support: the crash report signals every thread
  with a raw tgkill syscall, which Fil-C refuses, so a crash report after
  a failed assertion ended in a Fil-C panic instead.

